#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065232} :activity/id #uuid "6b61e9db-9682-5425-ad74-a269bcaceb50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049026 :topic/id #uuid "d49a6c77-a56c-5955-b68f-69307587aa23" :topic/math-academy-id 352} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065341 :tutorial/content "A **perfect square trinomial** is a three-term expression that is the result of squaring a binomial.\n\nFor example, the expression\n$x^2 + 6x + 9$\nis a perfect square trinomial because it is the result of squaring the binomial $(x+3)$, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 6x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo factor a perfect square trinomial, we have to figure out what binomial it is the square of. We can often do this by relating the perfect square trinomial to the **square of a sum**:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}2} {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2+6x+9$ using the square of sum formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n$x^{2} + 6x + 3^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$x^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2}$\n3. Factor the trinomial using the \"square of a sum formula\". In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{3}}$:\n$(x + 3)^{2}$\n\nThis method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial. We will learn more general methods in the future." :tutorial/id #uuid "c04b9f03-f29f-5feb-8d13-7de9fc378a34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1596 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c96b380f-2e0a-5243-a862-5219272b92d7" :step/math-academy-id 4226 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064325 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063960 :question/id #uuid "d496f23e-6ef7-5ec2-bb6c-054759a5b033" :question/math-academy-id "e-1327"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373d4ad5-ef39-5f03-bba4-d9be901e2587" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064746}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Sum"} :step/id #uuid "d98ff5d5-8ca1-5834-941d-13d2afb9da6f" :step/math-academy-id 4227 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065256}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064326 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063961 :question/id #uuid "29cbd59e-9eff-5439-9551-4520f68707b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1328"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "457399ac-1ede-551f-968b-9ef270a67d0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064745}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"} :step/id #uuid "1253a4a8-20c7-58c3-9e8e-893e406761f2" :step/math-academy-id 4230 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064327 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063962 :question/id #uuid "2fb03f0f-25c2-57a6-9e33-da837af2f339" :question/math-academy-id "e-1329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48f257af-1f00-5cf9-89cd-3d44d21482ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064960}] :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Trinomial with Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "59f4cd9a-a9f4-5779-b601-96d0642330dd" :step/math-academy-id 4232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065345 :tutorial/content "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the **square of a difference**:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}2} {\\color{blue}a}x + {\\color{blue}a}^2 = (x - {\\color{blue}a})^2\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $x^2-10x+25$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write the constant term as a perfect square:\n$x^{2} - 10x + 5^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 5x + 5^{2}$\n3. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{5}}$, and we get\n$(x - 5)^{2}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "70396264-e3c3-50d9-94a4-95e8017dbfde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1855 :tutorial/title "Factoring a Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"} :step/id #uuid "5e99daa1-dbe7-5fe2-bda6-1e302fb1a276" :step/math-academy-id 6169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065234}}] :activity/title "Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064441}]}
